window.docContents[201].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Equivalent-definition-of-tensor-product-via-free-abelian-group/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___11___-Tensor-products--Equivalent-definition-of-tensor-product-via-free-abelian-group":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products","header":"Equivalent definition of tensor product via free abelian group","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Equivalent-definition-of-tensor-product-via-free-abelian-group/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___11___-Tensor-products--Equivalent-definition-of-tensor-product-via-free-abelian-group"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___1_irreducible_indecomposable":{"contents":"A nonzero representation V of A is said to be irreducible if its only subrepresentations\nare 0 and V itself, and it is said to be indecomposable if it cannot be written as a\ndirect sum of two nonzero subrepresentations. Obviously, irreducible implies indecomposable, but\nnot vice versa.\n\nTypical problems of representation theory are as follows:\n\n(1) Classify irreducible representations of a given algebra A.\n\n(2) Classify indecomposable representations of A.\n\n(3) Do (1) and (2) restricting to finite dimensional representations.\n\nAs mentioned above, the algebra A is often given to us by generators and relations. For\nexample, the universal enveloping algebra U of the Lie algebra \\mathfrak{sl}(2) is generated\nby h, e, f with defining relations\n\n(2.1.1) \\qquad he - eh = 2e, \\quad hf - fh = -2f, \\quad ef - fe = h.\n\nThis means that the problem of finding, say, N-dimensional representations of A reduces to\nsolving a bunch of nonlinear algebraic equations with respect to a bunch of unknown N \\times N\nmatrices, for example system (2.1.1) with respect to unknown matrices h, e, f.\n\nIt is really striking that such, at first glance hopelessly complicated, systems of equations can\nin fact be solved completely by methods of representation theory! For example, we will prove the\nfollowing theorem.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.1. What is representation theory?\u0009Irreducible and indecomposable representations; typical problems of representation theory","header":"Irreducible and indecomposable representations; typical problems of representation theory","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___1_irreducible_indecomposable"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Basis-dependent-definition-of-universal-enveloping-algebra/#Chapter2___Remark2___9___10":{"contents":"Remark 2.9.10. This is not a very good definition since it depends on the choice of a basis. Later we will give an equivalent definition which will be basis-independent.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Basis-dependent definition of universal enveloping algebra","header":"Basis-dependent definition of universal enveloping algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Basis-dependent-definition-of-universal-enveloping-algebra/#Chapter2___Remark2___9___10"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras","header":"Universal enveloping algebra of sl(2)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Section-5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___26___-Artin___s-theorem--Section-5___26___-Artin___s-theorem":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem","header":"Section 5.26: Artin's theorem","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Section-5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___26___-Artin___s-theorem--Section-5___26___-Artin___s-theorem"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___7___-Virtual-representations/Virtual-representation-with-inner-product-1-and-positive-dimension-is-irreducible/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___7___-Virtual-representations--Virtual-representation-with-inner-product-1-and-positive-dimension-is-irreducible--Virtual-representation-with-inner-product-1-and-positive-dimension-is-irreducible--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An integral linear combination of pairwise nonisomorphic simple characters with self-inner\nproduct one and positive degree has exactly one coefficient equal to one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_before_Lemma5.7.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Lemma5.7.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.7. Virtual representations\u0009Virtual representation with inner product 1 and positive dimension is irreducible\u0009Virtual representation with inner product 1 and positive dimension is irreducible\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___7___-Virtual-representations/Virtual-representation-with-inner-product-1-and-positive-dimension-is-irreducible/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___7___-Virtual-representations--Virtual-representation-with-inner-product-1-and-positive-dimension-is-irreducible--Virtual-representation-with-inner-product-1-and-positive-dimension-is-irreducible--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/#chapter-06___section-6-5":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations","header":"6.5. Gabriel's theorem","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/#chapter-06___section-6-5"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Section-6___8-heading-and-setup-for-proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Section6___8_heading___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Section 6.8 heading and setup for proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Section 6.8 heading and setup for proof of Gabriel's theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Section-6___8-heading-and-setup-for-proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Section6___8_heading___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Short-exact-sequence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Short-exact-sequence":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology","header":"Short exact sequence","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Short-exact-sequence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Short-exact-sequence"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Section-9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___3___heading-1":{"contents":"Let A be a finite dimensional algebra with simple modules M_i, i = 1, \\ldots, n, and projective covers P_i. Let c_{ij} := \\dim \\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_i, P_j) = [P_j : M_i].\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Section 9.3: The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Section 9.3: The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra","header":"9.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Section-9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___3___heading-1"}});